    Self-review:                                                                
    - [edge_case] bst.py:BinarySearchTree.insert: Duplicate detection relies on 
    neither a<b nor b<a. For values with partial orders (e.g. float NaN) every  
    comparison is False, so NaN is treated as a duplicate of any value and      
    silently                                                                    
    ignored, and a NaN in the tree makes search of other values route           
    incorrectly.                                                                
    Spec says values are mutually comparable, but NaN violates total ordering. →
    Document in the class docstring that values must form a total order (NaN    
    unsupported), or reject NaN in insert/search with ValueError. Add a test    
    covering the chosen behavior.                                               
    - [edge_case] bst.py:BinarySearchTree.search: search on an empty tree and   
    with                                                                        
    values of mixed incomparable types raises TypeError only when a comparison  
    happens; the empty-tree path never compares, so behavior is inconsistent    
    across                                                                      
    trees (e.g. insert 1 then search 'a' raises, but search 'a' on empty returns
    False). → Either document this or validate types on first insert; add a test
    asserting the chosen behavior for mixed types.                              
    - [edge_case] bst.py:BinarySearchTree.__init__ / _size: _size is maintained 
    but                                                                         
    never exposed or tested; a faulty count would go unnoticed. Duplicate insert
    must not increment it. → Add a test (or internal check) that inserting      
    duplicates leaves the size unchanged, e.g. insert 7 twice and assert        
    tree._size                                                                  
    == 1.                                                                       
    - [edge_case] test_bst.py:test_deep_tree_no_recursion_error: A chain of 5000
    inserts is O(n^2) (~12.5M comparisons) and may be slow in the test suite; it
    also does not verify that the tree is actually deep, only that operations   
    succeed. → Keep it but assert the chain depth via a helper walking          
    left/right                                                                  
    links, or reduce n to 2000 while still exceeding the default recursion limit
    of                                                                          
    1000 for the search path.                                                   
    - [edge_case] bst.py:insert / search with None value: None is not comparable
    with ints; inserting None into an empty tree succeeds, but any later insert 
    raises TypeError, leaving the tree in a partially-usable state with no      
    validation. → Reject None explicitly with a TypeError or ValueError in      
    insert,                                                                     
    and add a test for it.                                                      
                                                                                
    Address every item with a code change or a new test, keep all tests passing,
    and                                                                         
    run each test file with exec_python before finishing.                       
