self-review: 7 critique items, revised test_bst.py
    bug test_bst.py:_depth: _depth reads tree._root and node.left/node.right directly; if bst.py names these attributes differently (e.g. _head, or stores children in a dict) every depth assertion raises AttributeError and fails the 2000-element tests for a reason unrelated to recursion.
    edge_case test_bst.py:test_string_values / test_search_empty_tree_returns_false: Tests assume search on empty tree never compares, and that mixed-type comparison raises TypeError on insert; if the implementation wraps or converts comparison errors, these expectations break silently and the mixed-type test is the only guard.
    bug bst.py insert/search (recursive implementation assumed): The 2000-element sorted and reverse-sorted tests fail with RecursionError if insert or search or inorder are recursive; the default limit is 1000, so a recursive implementation cannot pass without a fix.
    edge_case test_bst.py:test_inorder_negative_zero_is_duplicate_of_first_inserted: Asserts 0.0 is retained over -0.0 and that the first-inserted object is kept; this depends on the implementation not replacing on equal, which is an unspecified detail beyond the stated contract.
    edge_case test_bst.py:test_inorder_bool_equal_to_int_is_dropped / test_inorder_int_then_equal_float_keeps_first_type: These pin down type-retention behaviour for equal-but-different-type values, which the spec does not define; they can fail for valid implementations.
    edge_case test_bst.py:test_none_rejected_on_insert_and_search / test_nan_rejected_on_insert_and_search: The spec does not say None or NaN must raise; these tests enforce an unrequested validation contract and will fail a correct-but-permissive implementation, and they also fail if bst.py lacks such checks.
    edge_case test_bst.py:test_inorder_random_permutations_match_sorted_set: Uses tree._size, which is an internal counter; if bst.py tracks size differently (or not at all), this fails spuriously. Also _size is asserted to equal the distinct count after duplicates.
